WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.000
Celebrate people's history.

00:02.000 --> 00:05.000
Reparations now, reparations won.

00:05.000 --> 00:10.700
From 1972 to 1991, a former Chicago police commander, John

00:10.700 --> 00:15.500
Birch and his men systematically tortured over 119 African

00:15.500 --> 00:17.500
American men and women.

00:17.500 --> 00:20.500
Following decades of struggle by groups such as black people

00:20.500 --> 00:24.700
against police torture in 2013, the Chicago torture justice

00:24.700 --> 00:28.500
memorials introduced a reparations ordinance in City Hall

00:28.500 --> 00:31.700
demanding holistic reparations for torture survivors and their

00:31.700 --> 00:32.700
families.

00:32.700 --> 00:37.200
With pressure from grassroots organizing on May 6, 2015,

00:37.200 --> 00:40.900
Chicago became the first US municipality to provide

00:40.900 --> 00:44.500
reparations for racially motivated police violence.

00:44.500 --> 00:47.900
The additional fifth star on the Chicago flag represents the

00:47.900 --> 00:51.200
devastating impact that torture has had on survivors and

00:51.200 --> 00:52.400
their communities.

00:52.400 --> 00:55.300
So for this piece, I was thinking about all the different

00:55.300 --> 01:00.700
people who led the fight for reparations and what really struck

01:00.700 --> 01:04.400
me the most was the mothers of survivors who fought for

01:04.400 --> 01:07.800
reparations for their for their sons.

01:07.800 --> 01:12.300
So here in the forefront is Mary L Johnson, who was the mother

01:12.300 --> 01:14.800
of a birch torture survivor.

01:14.800 --> 01:19.700
Who is the mother of a birch torture survivor and she really

01:19.700 --> 01:21.700
let the fight she filed the lawsuit.

01:21.700 --> 01:27.100
She was like was fighting so hard and people joined her and

01:27.100 --> 01:32.600
